### What is the single-supertype rule for anonymous classes?

The grammar of an anonymous class is `new SuperType(args) { body }`, where `SuperType` is a single class name or a single interface name. There is no syntactic slot for an `extends` clause combined with an `implements` clause. The compiler allows exactly one supertype.

```java
package com.bank.service;

import java.math.BigDecimal;

public class SingleSupertypeDemo {

    interface Auditable {
        String auditTrail();
    }

    static abstract class AccountProcessor {
        abstract BigDecimal process(BigDecimal amount);
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        // OK: one interface
        Auditable audit = new Auditable() {
            @Override
            public String auditTrail() {
                return "Audit entry for Priya's account";
            }
        };

        // OK: one class
        AccountProcessor processor = new AccountProcessor() {
            @Override
            BigDecimal process(BigDecimal amount) {
                return amount.multiply(new BigDecimal("1.05"));
            }
        };

        System.out.println(audit.auditTrail());
        System.out.println(processor.process(new BigDecimal("100000")));
    }
}
```

```text
Audit entry for Priya's account
105000.00
```

### Why can an anonymous class not do both at once?

The anonymous syntax `new Type() { }` names exactly one type. There is no place to write `extends AccountProcessor implements Auditable` in that expression. A named class has separate `extends` and `implements` clauses, but the anonymous form was deliberately kept simple with only one supertype slot. This prevents ambiguity about which constructor to call and keeps the inline expression readable.

```java
// This does NOT compile:
// Auditable a = new AccountProcessor() implements Auditable { };
// There is no syntax for it.
```

### How does transitive interface implementation work?

An anonymous object can end up being both a subclass instance and an interface implementer, but only when the single class being extended already implements that interface. The interface comes from the base class's type hierarchy, not from the anonymous declaration.

```java
package com.bank.service;

import java.math.BigDecimal;

public class TransitiveDemo {

    interface Schedulable {
        void schedule();
    }

    static abstract class SettlementTask implements Schedulable {
        abstract BigDecimal settle();
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        // Anonymous class extends SettlementTask
        // It is also Schedulable because SettlementTask implements Schedulable
        SettlementTask task = new SettlementTask() {
            @Override
            public void schedule() {
                System.out.println("Scheduled settlement for National Bank of India");
            }

            @Override
            BigDecimal settle() {
                return new BigDecimal("5000000");
            }
        };

        Schedulable s = task; // legal: transitive interface
        s.schedule();
        System.out.println("Settled: " + task.settle() + " INR");
    }
}
```

```text
Scheduled settlement for National Bank of India
Settled: 5000000 INR
```

### What is the combined-interface workaround?

Define a single interface that extends multiple interfaces, then implement that combined interface anonymously. Interfaces can extend multiple interfaces, so this composes cleanly while respecting the one-supertype rule.

```java
package com.bank.service;

import java.math.BigDecimal;

public class CombinedInterfaceWorkaround {

    interface Auditable {
        String auditTrail();
    }

    interface Comparable2 {
        int compareByAmount(BigDecimal other);
    }

    // Combined interface extends both
    interface AuditableComparable extends Auditable, Comparable2 {
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        AuditableComparable entry = new AuditableComparable() {
            private final BigDecimal amount = new BigDecimal("75000");

            @Override
            public String auditTrail() {
                return "Trade entry: " + amount + " INR";
            }

            @Override
            public int compareByAmount(BigDecimal other) {
                return amount.compareTo(other);
            }
        };

        System.out.println(entry.auditTrail());
        System.out.println("Compare to 50000: " + entry.compareByAmount(new BigDecimal("50000")));
    }
}
```

```text
Trade entry: 75000 INR
Compare to 50000: 1
```

### When should you use a named class instead?

When you must extend a concrete class and implement a new interface that the class does not already carry, the anonymous form cannot express it. Use a named class, which also improves testability and readability in a bank's codebase.

```java
package com.bank.service;

import java.math.BigDecimal;

public class NamedClassExample {

    interface Exportable {
        String export();
    }

    static class LedgerEntry {
        String id;
        BigDecimal amount;

        LedgerEntry(String id, BigDecimal amount) {
            this.id = id;
            this.amount = amount;
        }
    }

    // Named class: extends class AND implements interface
    static class ExportableLedgerEntry extends LedgerEntry implements Exportable {
        ExportableLedgerEntry(String id, BigDecimal amount) {
            super(id, amount);
        }

        @Override
        public String export() {
            return id + "," + amount;
        }
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        ExportableLedgerEntry entry =
                new ExportableLedgerEntry("TXN-5001", new BigDecimal("320000"));
        System.out.println(entry.export());
    }
}
```

```text
TXN-5001,320000
```

### How is this different from a named inner class?

A named inner class can appear in an `extends` and `implements` clause just like a top-level class. An anonymous class cannot, because it has no declaration syntax for those clauses. A named inner class is also reusable by name and testable in isolation.

| Feature | Anonymous Class | Named Inner Class |
|---|---|---|
| Supertypes | Exactly one | One class + multiple interfaces |
| Reusable by name | No | Yes |
| Testable in isolation | Difficult | Easy |
| Constructor | No | Yes |

### What are common mistakes with the single-supertype rule?

Developers sometimes try to add an interface in the anonymous declaration, which does not compile.

```java
// WRONG: trying to extend and implement in one anonymous class
// AccountProcessor p = new AccountProcessor() implements Auditable {
//     ... // COMPILE ERROR: syntax not supported
// };

// CORRECT: use the combined-interface workaround or a named class
```

### What should a senior engineer know?

In a banking production codebase, the single-supertype restriction is actually a nudge toward better design. A settlement handler that must be both a domain `SettlementStrategy` and a Spring `ApplicationListener` cannot be an anonymous class satisfying both independently. It becomes a named class, which is easier to test, document, and review. Senior engineers embrace this restriction because it pushes complex, multi-contract types into named, well-structured classes.

```java
package com.bank.service;

import java.math.BigDecimal;

// Production pattern: named class with multiple contracts
public class SettlementHandler
        extends SettlementStrategy
        implements ApplicationListener<SettlementEvent>, Auditable {

    @Override
    public BigDecimal execute(BigDecimal amount) {
        return amount; // settlement logic
    }

    @Override
    public void onApplicationEvent(SettlementEvent event) {
        // handle event
    }

    @Override
    public String auditTrail() {
        return "SettlementHandler audit";
    }
}
```
